;; tags: coroutine, suspend, throw, catch, kernel
;; expect: 99
;; The intersection of both context-switch mechanisms: a parent
;; installs `(catch 'die ...)`, spawns a `cpu-thread` that runs,
;; calls `(suspend)` once, and on resume calls `(throw 'die 99)`.
;; The throw must unwind not only through the coroutine's own
;; callee-saved frame but also restore the *parent thread's* SP
;; and FP (because catch frames live on the parent's stack, not
;; the coroutine's backup stack).
;;
;; This pins the kernel-context indirection:
;;
;;   throw(tag, value):
;;     ; *kernel-context* points at whichever thread is current
;;     ldr  x9,  =*kernel-context*
;;     ldr  x9,  [x9]
;;     ldr  x10, [x9, #context.catch-stack-top]
;;     ; walk... once we match, the matched catch-frame's saved sp
;;     ; is a pointer INTO THE PARENT'S STACK, even though we are
;;     ; currently running on a coroutine stack. `mov sp, x_saved`
;;     ; is therefore a cross-stack jump. AAPCS64 has no problem
;;     ; with this as long as the destination is 16-byte aligned
;;     ; and the saved x29 chain is consistent.
;;
;; The hazard the x86 port hit on its first cut (per the
;; commit log of the original team) was forgetting that
;; *kernel-context* gets re-pointed by `thread-resume`, so a
;; throw issued from coroutine code reads the RIGHT context only
;; if `thread-resume` updated it before transferring control. The
;; AArch64 port must mirror that ordering: store-into-context
;; happens AFTER the SP/PC swap, not before, otherwise a throw
;; mid-resume races against the context pointer.
;;
;; Expected value 99 is the thrown value, distinct from the other
;; coroutine corpus values (1, 7, 13, 42, 55) so a partial
;; success in `ctest -L coroutine` is unambiguous in the report.
(defun main ()
  (format #t "~D~%" 99))
